---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";

const site = "https://cognitsystem.com";
const slug = "erp-free-trial-nz";
const url = `${site}/blog/${slug}/`;

const title = "ERP Free Trial — How to Try an ERP Before You Buy (NZ) | Cognit";
const description =
  "Almost no ERP gives you a real free trial, and the ones that do rarely give you anything useful. Here's how to properly de-risk an ERP decision in New Zealand — what to insist on seeing, what to ask, and the questions that expose a bad fit early.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Do ERPs offer a free trial?",
    a: "Almost none do, and it's worth understanding why rather than treating it as a red flag. An ERP only becomes useful once your products, customers and prices are in it, which is days of work — so a self-serve trial you set up alone tends to prove nothing. We don't run one either. What we do instead is show you the real system with data shaped like yours, and tell you plainly what it doesn't do.",
  },
  {
    q: "If there's no trial, how do I avoid buying the wrong thing?",
    a: "Insist on three things. See the real product rather than a slide deck, with data that resembles your business. Ask the vendor directly what their system does not do — a straight answer is the single most useful signal you will get. And ask to speak to a client actually running it. A vendor who dodges any of those three is telling you something.",
  },
  {
    q: "What should I put an ERP through before committing?",
    a: "Your own workflow, not a generic one. Walk through your real products and customer pricing, place an order the way a customer would, receive stock against a purchase order, raise an invoice and check the reports actually tell you what you need. Bring your most awkward edge case — the customer with the strange pricing deal, the product that ships in two units — because that is where systems break.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is it risky to buy an ERP without trying it first?",
    a: "Yes — committing to an ERP you've only seen in a polished demo is one of the most expensive mistakes a distributor can make, because switching later costs time and money. The way to remove that risk isn't a trial login; it's insisting on seeing the real product with your kind of data, getting an honest list of what it doesn't do, and talking to someone already running it.",
  },
];

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "ERP free trial: how to try an ERP before you buy",
  description,
  author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Cognit System", url: site },
  publisher: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "Cognit System",
    logo: { "@type": "ImageObject", url: `${site}/logo.png` },
  },
  datePublished: "2026-06-20",
  dateModified: "2026-06-20",
  mainEntityOfPage: { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": url },
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
  })),
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path={`/blog/${slug}`} schema={[articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
  <Header />

  <main class="bg-cog-stone text-cog-slate">
    <!-- HERO (photographic) -->
    <section class="orbs relative isolate overflow-hidden">
      <img src="/images/warehouse-worker.jpg" alt="" aria-hidden="true" class="absolute inset-0 -z-10 h-full w-full object-cover" />
      <div class="absolute inset-0 -z-10 bg-cog-ink/82"></div>
      <div class="dotgrid absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-40"></div>
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-20 pb-20 text-center sm:px-8 sm:pt-28 hero-rise">
        <span class="chip mx-auto border border-white/15 text-slate-200">Insights</span>
        <h1 class="mt-5 text-4xl font-extrabold leading-tight tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl">
          How to try an ERP before you buy
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-300">
          Almost nobody gives you a real trial — including us. Here's what to insist
          on instead, and the one question that exposes a bad fit fastest.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- BODY -->
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-16 sm:px-8">
      <article class="article">
        <p>
          Choosing an ERP is one of the biggest decisions a distribution business
          makes — and yet most vendors expect you to commit before you've ever used the
          real thing. You see a polished demo, sign a contract, pay an implementation
          fee, and only then find out whether it actually fits how you work. That's
          backwards. This article explains why ERP trials are rare, why a trial login
          often wouldn't help you anyway, and what to insist on instead — including
          how we handle it at <a href="/platform">Cognit</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Why most ERPs don't offer a free trial</h2>
        <p>
          Traditional ERPs are sold, not signed up for. The typical path is a sales
          call, a scoped demo, a quote and a paid implementation project that can run
          for months. There's rarely a "click here to try it" button, for a few
          reasons:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>They're heavy to set up</strong> — a generic enterprise ERP can't be switched on in an afternoon, so there's nothing to trial</li>
          <li><strong>The business model relies on lock-in</strong> — long contracts and migration costs make leaving hard, which weakens the incentive to let you test first</li>
          <li><strong>Implementation is where they earn</strong> — the configuration is bespoke, so there's no standard product to hand you for free</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          None of that is in your interest. It means you carry all the risk of finding
          out — after you've paid — that the system doesn't suit a distribution
          business.
        </p>

        <h2>Why "no trial" is a real risk</h2>
        <p>
          An ERP touches everything: ordering, stock, pricing, invoicing, accounts. If
          it's the wrong fit, you don't just lose the licence fee — you lose the
          implementation cost, the time your team spent, and the months it takes to
          unwind and switch again. Buying on the strength of a demo alone is one of the
          most expensive mistakes a distributor can make. A demo shows you the system at
          its best, in someone else's hands. A trial shows you the system in <em>your</em>
          hands, with <em>your</em> data.
        </p>

        <h2>Why a trial login often wouldn't help you anyway</h2>
        <p>
          Here's the uncomfortable part. An ERP is worthless until your products, your
          customers and your agreed prices are loaded into it — and that is days of
          work, not an afternoon. Hand someone an empty system for two weeks and they
          spend the fortnight doing data entry, then judge the product on an experience
          that has nothing to do with running it. That's why the vendors who do offer
          trials often see them go cold.
        </p>
        <p>
          What actually de-risks the decision is being able to:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Walk your own products and customer pricing through the system, rather than a sample catalogue</li>
          <li>Place an order through the <a href="/ecommerce">B2B online store</a> the way one of your customers would</li>
          <li>Receive stock, raise an <a href="/features/invoicing">invoice</a> and watch it flow through to the ledger</li>
          <li>Check the <a href="/features/reporting">reports</a> tell you what you actually need to know</li>
          <li>Push your most awkward edge case at it — the odd pricing deal, the two-unit product</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The one question that exposes a bad fit fastest</h2>
        <p>
          Ask the vendor: <strong>what does your system not do?</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          It sounds almost too simple. But a vendor who has a straight answer ready has
          thought honestly about their own product, and you can trust the rest of what
          they tell you. A vendor who changes the subject, or says "it does everything",
          is a vendor you will be arguing with in nine months about something you were
          sure you had been promised.
        </p>

        <h2>How we handle it at Cognit</h2>
        <p>
          We don't run a self-serve free trial, for exactly the reason above — an empty
          system proves nothing and wastes your fortnight. Instead:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>We show you the real product</strong>, loaded with data shaped like your business, so you're looking at your operation rather than a generic one</li>
          <li><strong>We publish what we don't do.</strong> Our <a href="/pricing">what's included</a> page lists what the system does today, what we're still building, and what it doesn't do at all — in public, before you ever talk to us</li>
          <li><strong>We'll put you in front of a client running it</strong>, so you hear it from someone with no incentive to sell you anything</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          None of that is as satisfying as a login you can click today. It is,
          however, considerably more likely to tell you the truth.
        </p>

        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <>
            <h3>{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </>
        ))}
      </article>

      <!-- CTA -->
      <div class="card-hairline mt-12 rounded-3xl bg-white p-8 text-center sm:p-10">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-cog-ink sm:text-3xl">Ask us what we don't do</h2>
        <p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-xl text-cog-slate">
          We'll show you the real system with data shaped like your business — and
          we've already published the gaps, so you can check before you call.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-3">
          <a href="/contact" class="btn-grad">Book a demo</a>
          <a href="/pricing" class="btn-ghost">See what's included</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
